{"id": "section-body-05966", "title": "Helminthic infections Neurocysticercosis (NCC)", "chapterId": "chapter-eight", "tocLevel": 4, "tocPath": ["APPROACH TO PATIENTS WITH EMERGENCY CONDITIONS", "NERVOUS SYSTEM DISEASE CONDITIONS", "Infections of the Nervous System", "Helminthic infections"], "blocks": [{"id": "body-05966", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading4", "text": "Helminthic infections Neurocysticercosis (NCC)", "runs": [{"text": "Helminthic"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "infections Neurocysticercosis (NCC)"}], "level": 4, "list": {"level": 2, "numberingId": "1653"}}, {"id": "body-05967", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "NCC is a neurologic infection caused by the larval stage of the tapeworm Taenia solium. In the developing world NCC is the most common cause of new onset acquired epilepsy among the adult population. Humans are the definitive hosts for this parasite, and swine are the intermediate hosts. The mature tapeworm develops in humans after they ingest live cysticercoses in undercooked pork. NCC develops when humans accidentally ingest eggs from fecal contaminated food.", "runs": [{"text": "NCC is a neurologic infection caused by the larval stage of the tapeworm Taenia solium. In the developing world NCC is the most common cause of new onset acquired epilepsy among the adult population. Humans are the definitive hosts for this parasite,"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "and"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "swine"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "are"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "the"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "intermediate"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "hosts."}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "The"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "mature"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "tapeworm"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "develops"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "in"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "humans"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "after"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "they"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "ingest"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "live"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "cysticercoses in undercooked pork.